<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John V of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a>–<a href="/years/1750">1750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as João V · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza">House of Braganza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John V of Portugal was a king of the first half of the eighteenth century. Gold and diamonds discovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> funded his extravagant court culture and building projects. His reign shows the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>'s dependence on Brazilian wealth in its later phase.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-v-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John V of Portugal" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Ranc · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D._Jo%C3%A3o_V_(1689-1750)_Hd.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was born on 22 October 1689 at Ribeira Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, the eldest surviving son of Peter II of Portugal and Maria Sophia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a>. His education was first supervised by women, in the custom of the Portuguese nobility. In December 1696 he was invested as Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a>. His mother died in 1699, and his aunt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a>, former queen consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland, then directed his education until her death in 1705. Peter II died on 9 December 1706, and John was acclaimed king a month later. In 1708 he married Archduchess Maria Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, his first cousin and the sister of Emperor Joseph I, a match meant to secure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>John ruled as an absolute monarch. He never convened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> and ignored meetings of the Council of State, preferring weekly private audiences with members of the three estates and a close circle of advisers. His main minister was Diogo de Mendonça Corte-Real, and the diplomat Luís da Cunha called him a despot. His foreign policy aimed at neutrality, except for Portugal's part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, which he inherited through the Methuen Treaty of 1703. He cultivated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>: a lavish entry for his ambassador in 1716 helped persuade Pope Clement XI to raise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> to a Patriarchate, and he later received the title Most Faithful Majesty.</p><p>Under John the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> became the base of Portuguese imperial power, as returns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> fell. Gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a>, collected through the royal fifth, filled the treasury. It paid for embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1715 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1716 and for the Royal Palace-Convent of Mafra, begun in 1717 as a modest convent for a hundred friars and enlarged, with gold produced by slave labour in the colonies, into a convent-palace for three hundred friars with a basilica and library. A Swiss resident remarked in 1726 that the king meant to build a second Escorial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John suffered three strokes in 1742, which left him paralysed on his left side. He remained active in government and would not let Crown Prince José take a role in it. He died on 31 July 1750, aged 60. Admirers called him the Magnanimous and the Portuguese Sun King. His reign marks the high point of Portugal's dependence on wealth from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John V of Portugal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Brazilian Gold Rush, sparked by the discovery of gold in Minas Gerais in the 1690s, and the discovery of diamonds around 1729 produced a second revenue surge that funded <b>João V</b>’s lavish court and, later, helped pay for the rebuilding of Lisbon after the 1755 Lisbon earthquake.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1728" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John V of Portugal"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4393 4234h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4393 4234h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1728">What was here in 1728? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
